# Per-device credentials for the kasa-tapo-services gateway.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in the real values for the
# devices listed in `devices.yaml`. The variable name pattern is:
#
#   <DEVICE_ID_UPPERCASE>_USER
#   <DEVICE_ID_UPPERCASE>_PASS
#
# So a device with `id: cam_lab499_west` reads:
#
#   CAM_LAB499_WEST_USER=...
#   CAM_LAB499_WEST_PASS=...
#
# Tapo cameras use TWO accounts:
#  * Tapo Camera Account (RTSP) - set via the Tapo phone app: Camera Settings
#    > Advanced Settings > Camera Account. This is what go2rtc uses to pull
#    the RTSP feed. We pass the same credentials to pytapo so privacy-mode
#    toggles and similar Tapo-app calls work too.
#  * ONVIF Account - set via the Tapo app: Camera Settings > Advanced
#    Settings > ONVIF (port defaults to 2020). This is what we use for PTZ
#    and presets. Configure as a SEPARATE variable pair using the suffix
#    `_ONVIF_USER` / `_ONVIF_PASS` if it differs from the Camera Account; if
#    omitted, the gateway falls back to the Camera Account credentials.
#
# Example (a Tapo C245D dual-lens camera):
# CAM_LAB499_WEST_USER=labcam
# CAM_LAB499_WEST_PASS=changeme
# CAM_LAB499_WEST_ONVIF_USER=labonvif
# CAM_LAB499_WEST_ONVIF_PASS=changeme
#
# Kasa plugs are credential-less for legacy HS-series (HS103, HS300). Newer
# Kasa devices that use the KLAP/Aes protocol require the TP-Link cloud
# account email + password:
# PLUG_HOTPLATE_USER=user@example.com
# PLUG_HOTPLATE_PASS=changeme
#
# Optional - override the gateway's bind/listen and go2rtc base URL.
# KASA_TAPO_HOST=127.0.0.1
# KASA_TAPO_PORT=8002
# GO2RTC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1984
# GO2RTC_CONFIG_PATH=/etc/go2rtc/go2rtc.yaml
